sec.p:
The basic idea of metacells is to partition cells into small groups of homogeneous cells, thereby removing the sparsity of RNA transcript counts associated with scRNA-seq technologies. Throughout the Article, we used metacell sizes of around 20–100 cells. For each metacell m, the absolute expression eg,m of a gene g is defined as the total number of transcript counts of this gene among all cells belonging to this metacell, normalized to the total number of counts, that is, \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$${e}_{g,m}={\sum }_{\{c\in m\}}{N}_{g,c}/({\sum }_{g}{\sum }_{\{c\in m\}}{N}_{g,c})$$\end{document}eg,m=∑{c∈m}Ng,c/(∑g∑{c∈m}Ng,c), where Ng,c is the number of the number of transcripts of gene g in the cell c.

